# Cline Rules for gdc-pm project

## Engineering Decision-Making
- State technical positions clearly and defend them. Do not immediately defer or present excessive alternatives when a clear best answer exists.
- If the user pushes back or asks a clarifying question, it is NOT a signal to abandon the recommendation — evaluate the substance of the pushback and either update the position with reasoning or hold it.
- Avoid listing 3+ options when one option is clearly superior. Pick the best option, explain why briefly, and proceed.

## Planning Efficiency
- Use ASCII wireframes, tables, and short structured lists to validate designs before writing code. These are cheaper to revise than 500-line implementations.
- Do not enter Act Mode and write large files until the layout and feature set have been explicitly approved by the user.
- When a plan spans multiple files, write a short implementation checklist and get agreement on the sequence before starting.

## O&G Domain Realism
- All scenarios, sensor ranges, fault physics, and unstructured data (mud logs, lab reports, shift notes) must be physically realistic for upstream oil & gas operations (drilling, completion, production).
- Do not invent sensor values, fluid properties, or operational procedures that an O&G professional would immediately recognize as wrong.
- Cite the relevant standard or physical principle when introducing a new fault scenario or sensor interpretation.

## Code Quality
- The current stack is FastAPI (Python) + vanilla HTML/JS. Vue.js via CDN is approved for the UI layer.
- Do not introduce build-pipeline dependencies (npm, webpack, React JSX) into the fault-trigger-ui container without explicit discussion and approval.
- Preserve the existing XGBoost health score models (*.ubj files). They are correct and validated. Do not retrain without an explicit reason.
- The existing /api/* endpoints must remain backward-compatible unless we explicitly agree to break them.

## Deployment Constraint
- `terraform/gke.tf` must NOT be applied — it would destroy the live cluster.
- All demo changes go into gke/fault-trigger-ui/index.html and gke/fault-trigger-ui/app.py.
- After changes: docker build → docker push → kubectl rollout restart.
